          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "5.1 Rational Fraction"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "1 Expressing a rational function as a sum of partial fractions is called:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "partial fraction resolution"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "long division"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "factorization"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "rationalization"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "Writing one rational function as a sum of simpler fractions (partial fractions) is partial fraction resolution.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "Expressing a rational function as a sum of partial fractions is called Partial Fraction Resolution."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "2 \\(\\dfrac{2}{x+1} + \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\\) equals:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2x - 1}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3x - 5}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3x + 3}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3x - 3}{(x+1)(x-2)}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "d",
            "explanation": "Common denominator \\((x+1)(x-2)\\): \\(2(x - 2) + 1(x + 1) = 3x - 3\\). Adding tops and bottoms separately (\\(\\frac{3}{2x-1}\\)) is wrong.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "In this unit we shall learn how to reverse the order in (i) and (ii)"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "3 Which of the following is an identity?",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(3x = 6\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(x^{2} - 4 = 0\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\((x + 2)^{2} = x^{2} + 4x + 4\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(x^{2} = x\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "Multiplying out \\((x + 2)^{2}\\) gives exactly \\(x^{2} + 4x + 4\\), so it holds for all \\(x\\). The others hold only for particular values (\\(x = 2\\); \\(x = \\pm 2\\); \\(x = 0, 1\\)).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "Identity: It is an equation which holds good for all values of the variable"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "4 \\(x^{2} - 5x + 6 = 0\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "an identity"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "a conditional equation, true only for \\(x = 2, 3\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "a conditional equation, true only for \\(x = -2, -3\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "true for no value of x"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "b",
            "explanation": "\\(x^{2} - 5x + 6 = (x - 2)(x - 3)\\), which is 0 only at \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = 3\\); at \\(x = 0\\) it gives \\(6 \\ne 0\\), so it is not an identity.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "Conditional equation: It is an equation in which two algebraic expressions are equal for particular values of the variable"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "5 The symbol \\(\\equiv\\) is read as:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\"is approximately equal to\""
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\"is not equal to\""
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\"is equivalent by row operations to\""
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\"is identically equal to\""
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "d",
            "explanation": "\\(\\equiv\\) is read \\\"is identically equal to\\\"; it marks an identity, true for all values of the variable.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "is read \"is identically equal to\""
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "6 Which of the following is a rational fraction?",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x} + 2}{x + 1}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x + 1}{0}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^{2} + 1}{x - 3}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{2^{x}}{x + 1}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "A rational fraction is \\(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\) with polynomials \\(P(x)\\), \\(Q(x)\\) and \\(Q(x) \\ne 0\\). \\(\\sqrt{x}\\) and \\(2^{x}\\) are not polynomials, and the denominator cannot be 0.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "is called a rational fraction. A rational fraction is of two types."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "7 Partial fraction resolution is an extremely valuable tool in the study of:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "calculus"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "matrices"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "geometry of triangles"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "statistics"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "The unit states it is an extremely valuable tool in calculus, to decompose a complex rational function into simpler fractions.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "1471ced8-12a0-50f0-b905-e94a9d2e6ac4",
              "quote": "It is an extremely valuable tool in the study of calculus"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "5.1.1 Proper Rational Fraction"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "8 Which of the following is a proper rational fraction?",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}}{x^{2} + 1}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{2x + 1}{x^{2} - 3}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^{3}}{x^{2} + 1}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{3x - 2}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "b",
            "explanation": "Proper: degree of numerator < degree of denominator. Only \\(1 < 2\\) qualifies; the others have \\(2 = 2\\), \\(3 > 2\\), \\(1 = 1\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "52d2df32-35dd-5d1e-96b8-f2fe9f134e27",
              "quote": "is called a Proper Rational Fraction if the degree of the polynomial"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "9 The degree of \\((x - 1)^{2}(x + 3)\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(3\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(4\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "b",
            "explanation": "The degree of a product is the sum of the degrees of the factors: \\(2 + 1 = 3\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "52d2df32-35dd-5d1e-96b8-f2fe9f134e27",
              "quote": "its degree is the sum of the degrees of its factors"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "10 The degree of the non-zero constant polynomial \\(5\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(0\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(5\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "not defined"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "\\(5 = 5x^{0}\\), so a non-zero constant has degree 0. Hence \\(\\frac{5}{x + 2}\\) is proper (\\(0 < 1\\)).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "52d2df32-35dd-5d1e-96b8-f2fe9f134e27",
              "quote": "a non-zero constant has degree 0, because"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "11 The fraction \\(\\dfrac{12x^{2}}{x^{3} + 1}\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "improper, because \\(12 > 1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "improper, because \\(2 < 3\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "neither proper nor improper"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "proper, because \\(2 < 3\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "d",
            "explanation": "Only the degrees count: numerator degree 2, denominator degree 3, so \\(2 < 3\\): proper. The coefficient 12 does not matter.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "52d2df32-35dd-5d1e-96b8-f2fe9f134e27",
              "quote": "The size of the coefficients does not matter."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "5.1.2 Improper Rational Fraction"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "12 Which of the following is an improper rational fraction?",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x - 1}{x^{2} + 4}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{x + 4}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + 4}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{x^{2}}{x^{3} - 8}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "Improper: degree of numerator \\(\\ge\\) degree of denominator. \\(2 = 2\\), so \\(\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + 4}\\) is improper; equal degrees count as improper.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "is called an Improper Rational Fraction if the degree of the polynomial"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "13 By long division, \\(\\dfrac{2x^{2} - 3x + 4}{x - 2}\\) equals:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(2x - 7 + \\dfrac{18}{x - 2}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(2x + 1 + \\dfrac{2}{x - 2}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(2x + 1 + \\dfrac{6}{x - 2}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(2x + 1 - \\dfrac{6}{x - 2}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "\\(2x^{2} \\div x = 2x\\); \\(2x(x - 2) = 2x^{2} - 4x\\); remainder \\(x + 4\\); \\(x \\div x = 1\\); \\(1(x - 2)\\); remainder \\(4 + 2 = 6\\). Check: \\((x - 2)(2x + 1) + 6 = 2x^{2} - 3x + 4\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "Any improper rational fraction can be reduced by division to a mixed form"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "14 When \\(x^{3} + 2\\) is divided by \\(x - 1\\), the remainder is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(3\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(-1\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "b",
            "explanation": "\\(x^{3} + 2 = (x - 1)(x^{2} + x + 1) + 3\\), so \\(\\frac{x^{3} + 2}{x - 1} = x^{2} + x + 1 + \\frac{3}{x - 1}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "the sum of a polynomial and a proper rational fraction"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "15 If \\(ax^{2} + bx - c = 3x^{2} - 5x + 2\\) for all \\(x\\), then \\(c\\) equals:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(-2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(-5\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(3\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "Equate the constant terms: \\(-c = 2\\), so \\(c = -2\\). (Also \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = -5\\).)",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "the polynomials have same degree and the coefficients of like powers of the variable in both the polynomials must be equal"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "16 When a rational fraction is separated into partial fractions, the result is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "a conditional equation"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "an inequality"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "an equation with no solution"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "an identity"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "d",
            "explanation": "The result is an identity: it is true for all values of the variable in the domain of identity.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "When a rational fraction is separated into partial fractions, the result is an identity"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "17 The domain of the identity \\(\\dfrac{2x^{2} - 3x + 4}{x - 2} = 2x + 1 + \\dfrac{6}{x - 2}\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "all real \\(x\\) except \\(x = 2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "all real \\(x\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "only \\(x = 2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "all real \\(x\\) except \\(x = -2\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "Both sides must be defined; the denominator \\(x - 2\\) is zero at \\(x = 2\\), so the identity holds for every \\(x \\ne 2\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "for which both sides are defined"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "18 Before resolving \\(\\dfrac{x^{2} + 3x + 4}{(x + 1)(x + 2)}\\) into partial fractions, we must first:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "put x = 0"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "multiply the numerator by x"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "divide, because the fraction is improper"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "nothing; it is proper"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "Numerator and denominator both have degree 2, so the fraction is improper; divide first: \\(1 + \\frac{2x + 2}{(x + 1)(x + 2)}\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "Equal degrees already make a fraction improper"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "19 If \\((p - 1)x^{2} + (q + 2)x + (r - 4) = 0\\) for all values of \\(x\\), then \\((p, q, r)\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\((-1, 2, -4)\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\((1, 2, 4)\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\((0, 0, 0)\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\((1, -2, 4)\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "d",
            "explanation": "A polynomial that is zero for every \\(x\\) has all coefficients 0: \\(p - 1 = 0\\), \\(q + 2 = 0\\), \\(r - 4 = 0\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "3d9823eb-a731-5085-ab4e-f4ec9307b4c0",
              "quote": "A non-zero polynomial of degree 3 can be zero for at most 3 values of"
