          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 3,
            "text": "5.1.3 Resolution of a Rational Fraction P(x)/Q(x) into Partial Fractions"
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "20 The first point in resolving \\(\\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\\) into partial fractions is to make sure that:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "degree of \\(P(x)\\) > degree of \\(Q(x)\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "degree of \\(P(x)\\) < degree of \\(Q(x)\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(P(x)\\) is a constant"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(Q(x)\\) has no linear factor"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "b",
            "explanation": "Step (i): the degree of \\(P(x)\\) must be less than that of \\(Q(x)\\); if not, divide and resolve only the remainder fraction.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "8b8c688b-1f4e-5e96-bbf4-28dacde02c2c",
              "quote": "must be less than that of"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "21 The correct form of the partial fractions of \\(\\dfrac{x + 5}{(x - 1)(x + 2)}\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{Ax + B}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{C}{x + 2}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{A}{(x - 1)(x + 2)}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{A}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{B}{x + 2}\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{A}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{B}{(x + 2)^{2}}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "c",
            "explanation": "Case I: each non-repeated linear factor gets one fraction with a constant on top.",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "8b8c688b-1f4e-5e96-bbf4-28dacde02c2c",
              "quote": "when Q(x) has only non-repeated linear factors"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "22 In \\(\\dfrac{x + 5}{(x - 1)(x + 2)} = \\dfrac{A}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{B}{x + 2}\\), the value of \\(A\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(-1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(6\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)"
              }
            ],
            "answer_id": "a",
            "explanation": "\\(x + 5 = A(x + 2) + B(x - 1)\\). Put \\(x = 1\\): \\(6 = 3A\\), so \\(A = 2\\).",
            "book_ref": {
              "topic_id": "8b8c688b-1f4e-5e96-bbf4-28dacde02c2c",
              "quote": "Each zero of a linear factor switches off all the other unknowns."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "checkpoint",
            "mode": "question",
            "prompt": "23 In the same identity, the value of \\(B\\) is:",
            "options": [
              {
                "id": "a",
                "text": "\\(1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "b",
                "text": "\\(-1\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "c",
                "text": "\\(2\\)"
              },
              {
                "id": "d",
                "text": "\\(3\\)"
              }
